          "num": "13.3",
          "heading": "Night Patrols and the Weeping Mother",
          "text": "Hazrat Umar (RA) was deeply committed to the welfare of the disadvantaged. He frequently patrolled the streets of Madina during the dark hours of the night to see for himself whether anyone was in distress. On one bitterly cold night, accompanied by his servant Aslam, he observed a distant fire in the desert outskirts. Approaching the tent, he discovered a poor widow surrounded by weeping children. A pot filled with water sat upon the burning fire.\n\nHazrat Umar (RA) greeted the woman and inquired why the children were crying so bitterly. The mother explained that they were weeping from unbearable hunger, and that she had placed stones in the boiling water merely to pretend that food was cooking until the exhausted children fell asleep from exhaustion. She added in distress: 'Allah will judge between us and Umar on the Day of Judgment, for he neglects us!'\n\nHearing these words, tears welled up in Hazrat Umar's eyes. Without revealing his identity, he hurried back to the state warehouse in Madina. He packed a large sack with flour, dates, fat, and clothing. When his servant offered to carry the burden, Hazrat Umar (RA) replied sternly: 'Will you carry my burden of sins on the Day of Judgment?' He hoisted the heavy sack upon his own back and walked back to the woman's tent. There, he personally kneaded the dough, blew upon the fire until the hearth glowed, cooked a nourishing meal, and fed the starving children until they laughed with joy. Before departing, he arranged a permanent state dole (pension) for the family from the Bait-ul-Mal.",
