        "title": "Opportunity: Dressed in Overalls and Disguised as Hard Work",
        "text": "Thomas Alva Edison famously observed: 'Opportunity is missed by most people because it is dressed in overalls and looks like work.' This insightful maxim cuts directly to the core of human achievement and beautifully complements Walter Malone's poem 'Opportunity'. Many people spend their lives daydreaming about sudden luck, inheritances, or windfall success, expecting fortune to arrive effortlessly like a king at their banquet. When opportunities actually present themselves in the guise of strenuous labor, early mornings, rigorous study, and unglamorous persistence, people often fail to recognize them.\n\nFirst and foremost, real opportunities rarely announce themselves with trumpets or fanfare. They arrive disguised as daunting problems demanding innovative solutions, difficult tasks requiring intense discipline, or unglamorous entry-level positions that test one's character. In Malone's words, Opportunity bids us to 'wake, and rise to fight and win.' The verb 'fight' signifies that success is not a passive gift but an active, arduous conquest. Those who wear the 'overalls' of dedication—welcoming dirty hands, sweat, and tireless repetition—are the ones who ultimately transform raw potential into monumental achievement.\n\nSecondly, the fear of failure and the ghost of past mistakes often blind people to the daily opportunities surrounding them. People mistakenly assume that because they faltered yesterday, today holds no promise. Malone reminds us that every morning presents an unwritten white sheet of paper. What transforms that blank page into a masterpiece of success is the willingness to roll up one's sleeves and labor with unwavering conviction.\n\nIn conclusion, opportunity is not a rare mystical visitor that knocks once and vanishes forever; it is an everyday companion waiting for those who possess the vision to see beneath its humble working attire. If we discard procrastination, stop weeping over past blunders, and boldly declare 'I can!', we will find that every sunrise offers abundant opportunities to achieve lasting excellence."
